er segment. The problem says \"Reject budgets... above total capacity.\" But also each segment has capacity, we must not allocate beyond capacity. The greedy algorithm should respect capacity: when a segment reaches its capacity, it should not be eligible for further allocation. So we need to check if allocations[i] < cap[i] before considering. Or after increment, if it hits cap, it's done. So in loop, for each segment consider only if alloc[i] < cap[i]. Also we need to ensure we don't exceed capacity. Since total budget <= total_cap, it's possible that we run out of segments before budget exhausted, but that would be infeasible? Actually total budget <= total_cap ensures capacity sum is enough, but greedy may cause early fill of some segments, leaving others with capacity but not allocated because scores are lower. That's okay; the remaining budget will be allocated to other segments as long as their scores are positive. But if all remaining eligible segments have score 0 (importance divided by something) but importance >0, score always >0. So loop should continue. However, if we ever have no eligible segment (all at capacity) while remaining>0, that would be infeasible because total budget <= total_cap but due to per-segment caps, we need to allocate to reach budget exactly. But it's possible that greedy allocation does not use all capacity of some segments; if total budget is exactly sum of capacities, then all segments will eventually reach capacity. If budget is less, some segments may not be fully used. The loop will end when remaining=0. No issue.\n\nBut we must ensure we don't allocate beyond capacity. So in each iteration, find max score among segments with alloc[i] < cap[i]. If none, then cannot allocate remaining; but given total budget <= total_cap, there should be at least one segment with remaining capacity. However, if the total remaining budget is positive and no segment has remaining capacity, that would imply we have allocated all capacity but still budget left, which contradicts total budget <= total_cap. So it's safe. But we still need to handle gracefully: if no eligible segment, raise ValueError or break? The spec says reject budgets above total capacity, so this shouldn't happen.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Use list of indices to preserve order.\n- Compute scores as float. Use division. Tie break by original index.\n\nReturn dict: {\"allocations\": allocations, \"by_name\": {segments[i]['name']: allocations[i] for i in range(n)}, \"total\": sum(allocations)}.\n\nEdge cases: Empty segments? Not given but handle.\n\nWrite code. Use no external imports.\n\nNow produce JSON. The output must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value being a string of Python code. The Python code must be properly escaped. We'll write the code as a multi-line string, then as a JSON string. Use triple quotes inside? We'll use double quotes and escape newlines and quotes.\n\nBetter to write the Python as a single line? No, we can keep newlines with \\n.\n\nWe'll produce the JSON manually or using Python? Since we are generating response, we need to ensure correct escaping.\n\nLet me write the Python code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    segments = case['segments']\n    budget = case['budget']\n    n = len(segments)\n    names = [seg['name'] for seg in segments]\n    importances = [seg['importance'] for seg in segments]\n    minima = [seg['minimum'] for seg in segments]\n    capacities = [seg['capacity'] for seg in segments]\n\n    total_min = sum(minima)\n    total_cap = sum(capacities)\n    if budget < total_min or budget > total_cap:\n        raise ValueError(\"Budget not in feasible range\")\n\n    alloc = list(minima)  # copy\n    remaining = budget - total_min\n\n    while remaining > 0:\n        best_score = -1.0\n        best_idx = -1\n        for i in range(n):\n            if alloc[i] < capacities[i]:\n                score = importances[i] / (alloc[i] + 1)\n                if score > best_score:\n               