oc, \\\"by_name\\\": by_name, \\\"total\\\": total}\"}\n\nNeed to escape double quotes inside the string: replace \" with \\\". Also backslashes if any. We have newlines as \\n. In JSON, we need to escape backslashes. Actually in JSON string, backslash is escape character, so we need to replace each backslash with \\\\ and then newline becomes \\\\n? No, we want the string to contain the literal newline? In JSON, you can either use \\n for newline or actual newline character. Usually JSON allows literal newline only if escaped? Actually JSON string cannot contain unescaped control characters, so newline must be escaped as \\n. So we write \\n. But then when embedding in JSON, we need to escape the backslash itself: so we write \\\\n. But careful: For the Python source, we want the actual newline characters in the string representation? When we output the JSON, the value of \"implementation\" is a string that will be parsed by the evaluator. That string should contain the Python source code. Newlines in the Python source can be represented as \\n in JSON string. So we need to write the Python code with actual newlines replaced by \\n. But we can also keep actual newlines inside the JSON string? JSON spec allows literal newline characters if escaped as \\n, but some parsers may accept literal newlines? It's safer to escape as \\n. However, for readability in our response, we might write the Python code with actual newlines, but then we need to ensure that the JSON is valid: a string with newline characters must have them escaped or be surrounded by quotes. Actually in JSON, a string can contain literal newline if it is escaped as \\n. If we put an actual newline inside the string, that is invalid JSON. So we must escape newlines as \\n.\n\nThus, we need to produce a JSON string where newlines are represented as \\n. Also quotes as \\\". So we'll write the Python code as a single string with \\n for newlines. Let me construct that.\n\nSimplify: write the Python code as